A||||14||mrfixit58||Fuel Injection Question for all you Tech guys.||||3||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||10-05-2000||04:34 PM||||I've been thinking about one of my future projects... a 65 SS Nova. Before you all start in on me, my parents gave me the car. I had my first date in that car, and traveled a lot of miles in it as a kid. So, I'm gonig to fix it up and keep it forever.<P>What does this have to do with Fieros? Well, the car came with a straight six (250 cid)and I've been thinking about rebuilding it. But, I would like to use fuel injection. Do any of you think it's possible to remove the fuel injection system from a 2.8 and adapt it to the old Chevy? If so, what is the minimum amount of equipment I need (i.e., no egr, cat, etc.)? The cubic in displacement is simular, I could probably rig a distributor, weld a bung for the O2 sensor, drill and tap for injectors (suggestions here), use the throttle body as a down-draft, etc.<P>Comments? Suggestions? Recommendations?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000001||JohnnyK||10-05-2000||04:45 PM||||yeah.. Keep the carb.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||10-05-2000||04:54 PM||||I agree...don't butcher this thing.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||10-05-2000||05:11 PM|||| mrfixit58 it is very possible but (I know you don't want to here this) you could install a Chevy small block v8 and buy a fuel injection kit for it like the one Edlebrock makes. You would than have a much better and more reliable engine than trying to adapt the fiero’s injection system to a straight 250 cu in v6. It would probably cost less too.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||10-05-2000||05:13 PM||||Thanks guys but... since I'm never selling, future value is not an issue. My plan is to keep the exterior body, and interior completely stock but substitute old drive train and drum brakes for a modern drive train and braking system. It's either fuel inject the six or install the TPI 350 I've got sitting in a parts car. A fuel injected stright six would be different enough to keep me from the V8. Maybe then the TPI V8 could find it's way into a Fiero!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||10-05-2000||05:23 PM||||88formula,<P>Thanks but, actually, it's got a V8 in it now (327 cid). I put one in it in the early 80s. I bought a wreaked 87 GTA with a TPI 350 and 700r4 thinking that would be the new drive train. It my still be but I got to thinking about the 6 cyl still sitting on my dad's barn floor and, since I'll already have a hotrod Fiero, a 69 Firebird 400 convertible, I think a "modernized" straight-6 would be kinda different... and kinda cool. Nothing's set in concrete yet so I really would like some opinions on the fuel injection conversion idea.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000006||terryk||10-05-2000||05:24 PM||||You'll only wish you had gone straight to the TPI V8.....||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||10-05-2000||06:13 PM||||Well, here's a brillianr observation....<P>If it <B>must</B> be  FI, make it throttle body injection, for simplicity's sake.      <P>...<B>so</B> brillianr, I can't even spell  "brilliant!"  That's why: <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-05-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000008||terryk||10-05-2000||06:38 PM||||Make it port.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||10-05-2000||06:47 PM||||Unless you can find a new manifold, which isn't likely, your only choice is TBI. The simplest system GM made was the 300 series TBI used in the older 4 cyl. That one only has MAP, Coolant temp, and O2 sensors to worry about. You'd have to use that aftermarket holley TBI unit to feed 2 more cylinders. Even then you'll likely need a diferant PROM for the ECM.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||10-05-2000||08:03 PM||||How are your fabrication skills?<BR>Make a new intake manifold from scratch.<BR>Injector placement and runner length/diameter would be your primary concerns. There is a book titled "Scientific Design of Intake and Exhaust Systems" that should have the info you need to maximize performance from your new manifold. I don't remember who wrote the book, but Amazon.com might have it.<P>Look here for details about adapting FI to a non-FI engine: <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>www.diy-efi.org</A> <P>Good luck. Don't let any of these gain- sayers get to you.||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||10-05-2000||09:02 PM||||mrfixit58 try these places. They specialize in making custom manifolds and fuel injection or EFI for people like you. <P>Force fuel injection<BR>Dept. HR12, 4285 S.W. 37th Ave., Miami, FL<BR>33133, 305/655-8300<P>Turbo City<BR>Dept. HR12, 1137 W. Katella, Orange, CA<BR>92867, 714/639-4933<P>These places may have a web-site so you will have to do a search.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfixit58||10-05-2000||10:16 PM||||Thanks guys,<P>If I do go with the SIX, I really want multi-port FI. The straight six has some unusual tendencies. Like running rich on the center two plugs and lean on the front and back. That's one reason why you seldom ever see a stright six idle smoothly. I don't think TBI would cure this problem.<P>Terryk - I may still go with the 350 TPI but I would really like to have something different. A  MPFI straight six would deff be diff.<P>My thought on the intake is to first see if I could modify a stock unit to accept the components. For MPFI motors, the intake is really just a port for the air. The problem is how to best position the injectors on the shaired ports. There are aluminum intakes available which may make modifications easier.<P>Assuming I could get all the mechanical stuff worked out, the next question is the computer. Would the fuel curve for the V6 work for the S6? <P>Thoughts?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000013||terryk||10-05-2000||10:42 PM||||Ok, picture this, I'm sitting here with a 65 Nova SS (sweet cars. 67 is my favorite)....<P>Just accept your destiny... ||||reg||6
Z||000014||StuGood||10-05-2000||10:53 PM||||You asked about drilling and tapping the intake manifold for injectors, I think.  FWIW, if you use the Fiero injectors, you won't tap anything - the injectors need smooth bores for their o-ring seals.  You'll need to get a different fuel rail, also.  <P>If the main reason you wanted FI is that you want to improve distribution of the A/F mixture, I'd go the roll-your-own glue together plastic intake manifold route (along with multiple carbs or TBIs).<BR>Reason: It's relatively cheap/easy to change things while you experiment like this.  Plus, it's wise to allow yourself an easy way back to OE configuration, for comparison purposes, IMHO.<P>Agree with the earlier post(s) about getting help from the pros.  Personally, I love to  benefit from others' experiences  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> !      ||||reg||4
